.TH SH-STATS 1
.SH NAME
stats \- shell script interface to stats(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load stats

.B id := ${stats new}
.br
.B stats
.I id
.B columns
.I n
.br
.B stats
.I id
.B data
.I value...
.br
.B stats
.I id
.B read
.I file
.br
.B stats
.I id
.B linefit
.I xcol ycol
.br
.B stats
.I id
.B polyfit
.I xcol ycol degree
.br
.B stats
.I id
.B clear
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.BR rows | cols | table
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.B column
.I n
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.BR mean | median | total | variance | stddev | popvariance | popstddev | min | max
.I col
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.B quantile
.I col p
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.BR covariance | correlation
.I col col
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.BR line | coefficients
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.B rsquared
.I xcol ycol
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.B predict
.I x
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.B ttest1
.I col mean
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.B ttest2
.I col col
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.B meanci
.I col confidence
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.BR normalcdf | normalquantile
.I x
.B }
.br
.B ${stats
.I id
.BR tcdf | tquantile
.I x df
.B }
.br
.B stats del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Stats
holds a table of numbers and gives
.IR stats (2)'s
summaries and fits of it from
.IR sh (1).
.PP
The table is what makes this worth having.
Say how many
.B columns
it has, then fill it with
.B data
or
.BR read ,
and everything afterwards names columns by number counting from 0 \- so
.B stats
.I id
.B linefit 0 1
fits the second column against the first.
.PP
.B Read
takes whitespace-separated numbers however they are laid out: the rows
are made by the column count and not by the newlines, so a file with one
number to a line reads the same as one with a row to a line.
.PP
.B Column
returns a whole column as a flat list and
.B table
the whole table, row after row \- which for a two-column table is
exactly what
.IR sh-plot (1)'s
.B append
wants for
.I x
and
.IR y .
.PP
.B Line
returns the slope, intercept,
.IR r \u\s-22\s+2\d,
the standard errors of the slope and the intercept, the residual
standard deviation and the number of points.
.B Coefficients
returns a polynomial fit's, highest power first.
.B Predict
evaluates whichever fit was done last, and
.B rsquared
scores it against the data \-
.B line
carries that number for a straight line, but a polynomial fit has no
such record and scoring it means evaluating it at every
.I x
and comparing.
.PP
.BR Ttest1 ,
.B ttest2
and the distributions return what
.IR stats (2)
returns:
.B ttest1
and
.B ttest2
give the statistic, the degrees of freedom and a two-sided
.IR p ,
and
.B meanci
gives the two ends of the interval.
The distributions need no table.
.SH EXAMPLE
Anscombe's first data set, in a file of
.I x y
pairs:
.IP
.EX
load std
load stats
d := ${stats new}
stats $d columns 2
stats $d read anscombe1
echo ${stats $d mean 0}		# 9
echo ${stats $d correlation 0 1}	# 0.8164
stats $d linefit 0 1
echo ${stats $d line}		# 0.50009 3.00009 0.66654 ...
echo ${stats $d predict 10.0}	# 8.001
stats del $d
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/stats.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-plot (1),
.IR sh-circuit (1),
.IR sh-control (1),
.IR stats (2),
.IR statstest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A column number the table does not have, a count of values that is not a
whole number of rows, a fit with more coefficients than points, a sample
that does not vary: each fails the command, the last three with the
message
.IR stats (2)
gives.
.SH BUGS
There is no test for this module in
.BR /appl/cmd .
.IR Stats (2)
underneath it is checked by
.IR statstest (1);
what is added here is the table, the file reader and the column
extraction, exercised by the example above and by
.BR /lib/demo/fitlab.sh .
.PP
.B Read
refuses a file containing a
.BR # ,
rather than half-supporting comments: the line endings are gone by the
time the words are seen, so everything after a
.B #
would otherwise be read as a number.
A file with headings or comments has to be stripped first.
.PP
The column count must be set before anything is put in the table and
cannot be changed afterwards.
.PP
.B Multifit
is not reachable from here, so a regression on several predictors needs
a Limbo program.
